#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0f7055d-9f45-5115-97f4-35d0c56008e0" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "1193941c-3af9-56fa-abf8-32a07228f46e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7862" :question/problem "A certain disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population. A test to detect the disease gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive for the disease?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a patient tests positive, and let $D$ be the event that the patient has the disease. Then $D'$ is the event that the patient does not have the disease.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D')P(D')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the disease is present in $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ of the population, we have\n$P(D) = \\dfrac{1}{5}, \\quad P(D') = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n- Since the test gives a positive result in $\\dfrac{10}{11}$ of the people with the disease, and $\\dfrac{3}{11}$ of the people without the disease, we have\n$P(A \\mid D) = \\dfrac{10}{11}, \\quad P(A \\mid D') = \\dfrac{3}{11}$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected patient tests positive is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= P(A \\mid D)P(D) + P(A \\mid D^{′})P(D^{′}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{10}{11})(\\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{3}{11})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{11} + \\frac{12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 + 12}{55} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/id #uuid "16c912f8-c999-5a82-a763-0fd4ccf492d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7863" :question/problem "In box $A$, there are $19$ marbles, $7$ of which are green. In box $B$, there are $21$ marbles, $8$ of which are green. The marbles from the two boxes are mixed, and one marble is randomly chosen from the mixture. What is the probability that the selected marble is green?" :question/worked-solution "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen marble is green, and let $A$ be the event that the chosen marble was in box $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the chosen marble was in box $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected marble is green, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G) = P(G\\cap A) + P(G\\cap A')\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(G\\cap A)$, we take the number of green marbles in box $A$ $(7)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes\n\n$$\n(19+21=40)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A) = \\dfrac{7}{40}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(G\\cap A')$, we take the number of green marbles in box $B$ $(8)$ and divide by the total number of marbles in both boxes $(40)$. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(G \\cap A') = \\dfrac{8}{40}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is green is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(G) &= P(G∩A) + P(G∩A^{′}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{40} + \\frac{8}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{15}{40} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "f13f7b98-a9fc-5b9f-ad44-59d5ead049a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7865" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components, where $70\\%$ of the components are produced by machine $A$, and the remaining $30\\%$ are produced by machine $B$. Of the components produced by machine $A$, $8\\%$ are defective, and of the components produced by both machines, $11\\%$ are defective. If a component produced by machine $B$ is randomly selected, what is the probability of being defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the selected component is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A$. Then $A'$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B$.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D) = P(D \\mid A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A')P(A')\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $70\\%$ of the components and $B$ the remaining $30\\%$, we have\n$P(A) = 0.70, \\quad P(A') = 0.30$.\n- Since $8\\%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ are defective, we have\n$P(D \\mid A) = 0.08$.\n- Since $11\\%$ of the components produced by both machines are defective, we have\n$P(D) = 0.11$.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D \\mid A')$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(D) &= P(D\\begin{vmatrix}A)P(A) + P(D \\mid A^{′})P(A^{′}) \\\\ 0.11|= (0.08)(0.70) + P(D \\mid A^{′})(0.30) \\\\ 0.110|= 0.056 + 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′}) \\\\ 0.3P(D \\mid A^{′})|= 0.054 \\\\ P(D\\end{vmatrix}A^{′})|= 0.18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $B$ is defective is $0.18$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07eb336-1ac8-5633-abaf-fcfca4c12bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Using The Law of Total Probability Stated in Terms of Intersections"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8239f60b-e995-5df9-b93f-9a23cb55fb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082587} {:answer/id #uuid "82a9c3ee-38ba-5e8a-8dea-3b67b37e4983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082588} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d0749-6a6b-53c8-8947-ff16be3f88d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082589} {:answer/id #uuid "e77cca3f-d984-57da-9024-6439d30bfbde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082590} {:answer/id #uuid "f1232724-9f5e-5918-9cbf-4d700c3dd774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e77cca3f-d984-57da-9024-6439d30bfbde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082590} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1cd9ae-fd55-5eb5-8235-301da4894ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082586}] :question/id #uuid "562b0d12-0300-5642-8de6-e35c968e2c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-88831" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by two machines, $A$ and $B$. In a particular random sample, $37$ of the components sampled were produced by machine $A$, and $43$ were produced by machine $B$. On inspecting the sampled components, it was found that $5$ defective components were produced by machine $A$ and $3$ defective components were produced by machine $B$. What is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5000a4b1-c0cb-59f3-9d45-6287fa8ac9b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9be8101-853c-5e90-bbf4-9dc1cc33fa29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082605} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4915a3-3555-5c10-9feb-6387352c3f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082606} {:answer/id #uuid "f717d1a1-6d07-5492-9bf5-c16f93fc198b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082607} {:answer/id #uuid "49a609c0-2540-5173-979a-90b54d9d39e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082608} {:answer/id #uuid "7f403e67-26d3-5981-abeb-ca9f111577e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f403e67-26d3-5981-abeb-ca9f111577e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082609} :answer-field/id #uuid "56eab7d9-7f5b-5bfe-b67b-db5ea19e8613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082604}] :question/id #uuid "b31dbf65-1f9b-5a11-91ad-3e7a54aebef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-89054" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA math teacher splits her calculus class into two groups, Group $A$ and Group $B$. Of the total students, $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$, and $65%$ are in Group $B$. If $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top grade is $0.265$, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
